<p>In cases where the body is preserved, <span class="customMeta" data-dictid="f0705db1901692777187acdc0f9e24">ethanol</span> can be detected in the <span class="customMeta" data-dictid="93943f6ee2169277720298325fd255">viscera</span> due to the <span class="customMeta" data-dictid="cc108d77f11692777202478d5c72bf">fermentation</span> of body fluids and tissues. This post-mortem production of alcohol can make its <span class="customMeta" data-dictid="723fdec66b1692777186e92f0c6fdf">detection</span> possible even if the person did not consume alcohol before death.</p>
<p><strong>Morphine (Options A), Aconite (Option B), and Arsenic (Option C)</strong> can only be detected if they were consumed by the person</p><br/><br/><div class="quiz-references">https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8840458</div>
